TI  - Closure of a Post-Transplant Bronchial Dehiscence With Endobronchial Fibrin 
      Sealant.
PG  - e193-e195
LID - S0003-4975(18)30579-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2018.03.077 [doi]
AB  - Airway complications after lung transplantation are well described and can lead 
      to significant morbidity and mortality. Treatment options for anastomotic 
      dehiscence include expectant management, placement of endobronchial stents, or 
      surgical repair. The use of fibrin sealant instilled by bronchoscopy to seal a 
      dehiscence has not been well described. Our patient is a 57-year-old man who 
      underwent orthotropic bilateral lung transplantation for end-stage chronic 
      obstructive pulmonary disease. He was found to have a partial bronchial 
      anastomosis dehiscence and was subsequently treated with endobronchial fibrin 
      sealant glue instillation. This case illustrates the successful use of 
      endobronchial fibrin sealant for bronchial anastomosis dehiscence.
