TI  - A video-assisted endotracheal suture technique for correction of distal tracheal 
      laceration after intubation.
PG  - 2073-5
LID - 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2011.11.018 [doi]
AB  - The incidence of tracheal laceration is 1 of 20,000 intubations. The most 
      frequently affected area is the posterior tracheal wall (membranous). Risk 
      factors include several forced attempts at intubation, inexperience of the 
      clinician, tracheal introducers (guidewires) that protrude beyond the tip of the 
      tube, and emergency procedures. Surgical treatment of tracheal lacerations can be 
      by a transtracheal suture technique or a right thoracotomy. Using the concept of 
      minimally invasive surgical procedures, we reported the treatment of 2 patients 
      with tracheal lacerations greater than 5 cm in the distal trachea that were 
      treated with endotracheal video-assisted suturing using a cervical incision.
