VI  - 84
IP  - 4
DP  - 2007 Oct
TI  - Giant carcinoid tumor mimicking pulmonary sequestration.
PG  - 1375-6
AB  - A 42-year-old woman who previously underwent two consecutive thoracotomies for a 
      lower lobe mass in her right lung was referred to our clinic for further 
      management. Both procedures were abandoned due to excessive bleeding. Computed 
      tomographic angiography demonstrated an infra-diaphragmatic systemic arterial 
      supply of the mass similar to pulmonary sequestration. However the lobe had a 
      normal venous drainage to the left atrium. Then a right lower lobectomy was 
      undertaken through a hemi-clamshell incision, and histopathology revealed an 
      atypical carcinoid tumor. The patient was discharged home after a satisfactory 
      postoperative period. She still remains disease free at 14 months follow-up.
