TI  - Lung Cancer in Morocco.
PG  - 103561
LID - S1556-0864(26)00014-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jtho.2026.103561 [doi]
AB  - Morocco faces a persistent gap between international standards for thoracic 
      oncology and local capacity, driven by limited diagnostics, uneven access to 
      molecular testing, fragmented procurement and reimbursement, and high 
      out-of-pocket costs. This manuscript synthesizes clinical experience, 
      service-level data, and policy analysis to map the continuum of lung cancer care 
      in Morocco, from symptom recognition and diagnostic pathways to multidisciplinary 
      decision-making, treatment delivery, and survivorship. We describe current 
      infrastructure and practice including concentrated computed tomography-guided and 
      endobronchial biopsy services, constrained pathology and immunohistochemistry 
      workflows, limited public-sector access to next-generation sequencing and 
      targeted therapies, uneven radiotherapy and surgical capacity, and fragmented 
      palliative and survivorship services. Finally, we evaluate recent policy 
      developments, including the 2023 rollout of Assurance de Maladie Obligatoire and 
      the establishment of the Agence Marocaine du Médicament et des Produits de Santé, 
      and identify persistent operational barriers that limit population-level benefit.
