TI  - Lobectomy as Lung Volume Reduction Surgery in a Patient With Previous 
      Contralateral Bilobectomy.
PG  - e261-e264
LID - S0003-4975(21)00178-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2021.01.033 [doi]
AB  - Lung volume reduction surgery is a well-established treatment for pulmonary 
      emphysema; however, lobectomy is performed infrequently in this indication. In a 
      48-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease at stage Global 
      Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease stage 3C, with previous lower 
      bilobectomy due to severe poststenotic pneumonia and multiple ineffective 
      endobronchial-valves placements, lower-lobe lobectomy as lung volume reduction 
      surgery was performed through anterolateral thoracotomy. Forced expiratory volume 
      in 1 second increased from 0.9l (31%) preoperative to 1.74l (59%) 
      postoperatively. This case demonstrates that in well-selected patients, lobar 
      resection for emphysema is feasible with good functional outcome even in patients 
      after previous contralateral anatomic resection.
