TI  - Endobronchial and endovascular management of bronchial compression by a thoracic 
      aortic aneurysm.
PG  - 273-4
LID - 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2011.12.026 [doi]
AB  - Thoracic aortic aneurysms are life threatening because of the risk of rupture. 
      Moreover, aneurysm enlargement can lead to additional complications, including 
      bronchial and esophageal obstruction. We report an 80-year-old man with a 7-cm 
      diameter thoracic aortic aneurysm resulting in near-complete left main bronchial 
      obstruction and significant dysphagia. He had a number of intensive care unit 
      admissions for respiratory failure and had lost more than 10 kilograms. Under 
      spinal anesthesia, he underwent endovascular thoracic aortic aneurysm repair. 
      Postoperatively, he had left main bronchial total obstruction treated with a 
      bronchial stent. He then recovered uneventfully and was discharged to home.
