TI  - Does hyperbaric oxygen therapy prevent airway anastomosis from breakdown?
PG  - 682-5
LID - S0003-4975(14)00821-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2014.03.055 [doi]
AB  - Ischemia with subsequent necrosis of anastomoses, after central airway resection 
      and reconstruction, remains a feared complication for thoracic surgeons and their 
      patients. To date, there is no evidence to support the use of hyperbaric oxygen 
      in the prevention of necrosis of airway reconstructions in humans. We present a 
      patient who underwent central airway surgery with postoperative ischemia of an 
      end-to-side anastomosis. Repeat visit to a hyperbaric oxygen chamber seemed to 
      prevent the anastomosis from subsequent necrosis and dehiscence with complete 
      healing as a result. In conclusion, hyperbaric oxygen treatment can be considered 
      when ischemia or necrosis is observed in central airway anastomoses during 
      postoperative bronchoscopic surveillance.
