DP  - 2008 Dec
TI  - Tube thoracostomy: the struggle to the "standard of care".
PG  - 2019-22
LID - 10.1016/j.athoracsur.2008.08.006 [doi]
AB  - Tube thoracostomy for thoracic injuries has been standard for only the last 40 
      years. Its theoretic roots trace back to World War II, where the goal of 
      treatment was restoration of intrathoracic organ function. Thoracentesis was used 
      to evacuate the hemopneumothorax resulting from chest trauma and that compromised 
      pulmonary function. Experience gained in military and civilian hospitals 
      contributed to the development of tube thoracostomy as an alternative treatment 
      for patients with chest trauma. Progress stalled due to technologic problems and 
      unacceptable complications associated with tube thoracostomy use during the 
      Korean War. Technology improved, however, as did the success of thoracostomy, and 
      it eventually become the standard of care, first in the civilian community and, 
      ultimately, in the Vietnam War.
