AB  - Congenital pulmonary venous stenosis is an uncommon congenital heart defect and 
      is a consequence of failed incorporation of the common right or left pulmonary 
      vein into the left atrium during embryologic development. Clinical manifestations 
      include progressive exertional dyspnea, cough, chest pain, and hemoptysis. In 
      this report, we describe a 37-year-old woman with known congenital unilateral 
      pulmonary venous stenosis and a unilateral hypoplastic right pulmonary artery who 
      presented with recurrent episodes of hemoptysis, requiring multiple 
      catheter-based interventions, and symptomatic pleural effusions, requiring 
      multiple large-volume thoracenteses. The patient underwent successful 
      video-assisted thoracoscopic pneumonectomy with resolution of symptoms.
